## Migration: `EventProcessor` → .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for `WaitOne` wakeup |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware |
| Race condition | Multiple enqueues with one `Reset()` can lose signals |

### Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** → `CancellationToken`
2. **Blocking call interruption** (`WaitOne`) → `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with token, or better: `Channel<T>`

---

### Migrated Code (Recommended: Channel-based)

```csharp
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Normal shutdown
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

### If You Must Keep Threads (synchronous alternative)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();          // signal no more items
        if (!_thread!.Join(5000))
            _cts.Cancel();               // force exit from blocking wait
        _thread.Join(1000);              // wait for cancellation to take effect
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Race in original** | One `Set()`/`Reset()` pair loses signals if multiple items enqueue before `Dequeue`. `Channel`/`BlockingCollection` fix this. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always implement `IDisposable` — leaking CTS leaks timers. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint (`ReadAllAsync`/`GetConsumingEnumerable`). If `Handle()` is long, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside it. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch at the top-level loop. Let it propagate from inner methods. |
| **Forgetting `CompleteAdding()`/`Writer.Complete()`** | Without this, the consuming loop never terminates gracefully — you'd rely solely on cancellation. |

The **Channel-based approach** is preferred for new .NET 8 code: it's async-native, allocation-efficient, and handles backpressure cleanly.
